    "text": "To determine the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n \\)-th roots of unity, we start by analyzing the given polynomial.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) in a more convenient form. Let \\( w = z^2 \\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[\nw^2 + w + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve this quadratic equation for \\( w \\). The roots of the quadratic equation \\( w^2 + w + 1 = 0 \\) can be found using the quadratic formula:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n\\]\nwhere \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 1 \\), and \\( c = 1 \\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the roots for \\( w \\) are:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad w = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\( w = z^2 \\). Therefore, we need to find the roots of \\( z^2 \\) from these values of \\( w \\).\n\n1. For \\( w = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\):\n\\[\nz^2 = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\nThe complex number \\( \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) can be expressed in polar form. The magnitude \\( r \\) is:\n\\[\nr = \\left| \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right| = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{-1}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{3}{4}} = \\sqrt{1} = 1\n\\]\nThe argument \\( \\theta \\) is:\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1} \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}/2}{-1/2} \\right) = \\tan^{-1}(-\\sqrt{3}) = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\nThus, \\( \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) in polar form is:\n\\[\n\\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} = e^{i \\frac{2\\pi}{3}}\n\\]\nTherefore, the roots \\( z \\) are:\n\\[\nz = \\pm \\sqrt{e^{i \\frac{2\\pi}{3}}} = \\pm e^{i \\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\]\n\n2. For \\( w = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\):\n\\[\nz^2 = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\nSimilarly, the magnitude \\( r \\) is:\n\\[\nr = 1\n\\]\nThe argument \\( \\theta \\) is:\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1} \\left( \\frac{-\\sqrt{3}/2}{-1/2} \\right) = \\tan^{-1}(\\sqrt{3}) = -\\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\nThus, \\( \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) in polar form is:\n\\[\n\\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} = e^{-i \\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\]\nTherefore, the roots \\( z \\) are:\n\\[\nz = \\pm \\sqrt{e^{-i \\frac{\\pi}{3}}} = \\pm e^{-i \\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\]\n\nCombining all the roots, we have:\n\\[\nz = e^{i \\frac{\\pi}{3}}, \\quad z = -e^{i \\frac{\\pi}{3}}, \\quad z = e^{-i \\frac{\\pi}{3}}, \\quad z = -e^{-i \\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\]\n\nThese roots are \\( 3 \\)-rd roots of unity because:\n\\",
